# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# CHAPTER 4. DATA MODEL  (the Data Model file vp/2_DataModel.vpp: class diagram with multiplicity, labels and ORM,
# the data model generated by Synchronize to Entity Relationship Diagram, the database generated from it)

chapter("Data Model")
p("This chapter is the Data Model file, vp/2_DataModel.vpp. It continues the Analysis model of Chapter 2: it keeps "
  "the same twenty classes and adds what a database needs, so that Visual Paradigm (VP) can turn the classes into "
  "tables. Section 4.1 is the class diagram with the additions; Section 4.2 the data model that VP's command "
  "Synchronize to Entity Relationship Diagram generated from it; Section 4.3 the database that VP's Generate "
  "Database wrote from the data model; Section 4.4 checks the tables against the normal forms. I did every step in "
  "the VP window, and the figures of this chapter are the dialogs I used.")

# ======================================================================= 4.1
h2("4.1. Class Diagram with Multiplicity, Labels and ORM")
p(f"Figure {nf()} is the class diagram of Chapter 2 drawn again with five additions. The classes, the operations "
  "and the association names are the ones of Chapter 2; the operations are not repeated, because a table stores data "
  "and not behaviour.")
dash("**Types and identifiers.** Every attribute has a type: Integer, String, Date, Double or Boolean. Every class "
     "that is not a subclass has an identifier attribute of type Integer, such as orderId, marked as the ID of the "
     "class. Money is an integer amount in VND.")
dash("**Multiplicity of attributes.** A required attribute has multiplicity 1 and an optional one 0..1, for example "
     "the brand of a product or the delivery date of a shipment that is still on its way. The multiplicity decides "
     "whether the column may be empty.")
dash("**Multiplicity and role name at both ends.** Every end of every association has a multiplicity and a role name "
     "(customer, orders, items ...). The role name says how one class reaches the other, and the multiplicity on the "
     "other side decides which table receives the foreign key.")
dash("**«ORM Persistable».** Every class has this stereotype, so VP maps it to a table. A class without it is ignored "
     "by Synchronize.")
dash("**Table per subclass.** The three generalizations, Product with its four kinds, Customer with CustomerVIP and "
     "CustomerNew, and Payment with PayCash and PayCredit, have the inheritance strategy Table per subclass (ORM "
     "Class Detail of the specification of the superclass). VP's default, Table per class hierarchy, would put a "
     "whole hierarchy into one table.")
landscape()
F_D1 = figure("docs/evidence/marked/D1_marked.png", "Class diagram with multiplicity, role names and ORM: the twenty classes of Chapter 2 with types, identifiers and the stereotype «ORM Persistable» (diagram D1)", width_cm=24.5, max_h_cm=11.5)
mark_notes(F_D1, [
    "Every attribute has a type and the class has an identifier (productId) and the stereotype «ORM Persistable»: VP makes a table of it.",
    "The subclasses keep the generalization; Product has the strategy Table per subclass (Figure 4.2), so each kind is a table keyed by ProductId.",
    "Every association end has a multiplicity and a role name (customer, orders, addresses); the table on the many side gets the foreign key CustomerId.",
    "All amounts of Order are Integer, whole VND; OrderItem, OrderEvent and Payment reach it through the role order and get the foreign key OrderId.",
])
portrait()
p(f"Table {nt()} lists the fourteen associations of Figure {F_D1}, with the role name and the multiplicity at each "
  "end. The six generalizations from a superclass to its subclasses have no label and no role names.", keep=True)
table("Associations of the data model with role names and multiplicities", [
    ["Label", "First end", "Second end", "Kind"],
    ["is parent of", "Category (role parent, 0..1)", "Category (role subcategories, 0..*)", "Association"],
    ["classifies", "Category (role category, 1)", "Product (role products, 0..*)", "Association"],
    ["has", "Customer (role customer, 1)", "Address (role addresses, 0..*)", "Association"],
    ["owns", "Customer (role customer, 1)", "Cart (role cart, 0..1)", "Association"],
    ["contains", "Cart (role cart, 1)", "CartItem (role items, 0..*)", "Composition"],
    ["is put in", "Product (role product, 1)", "CartItem (role cartItems, 0..*)", "Association"],
    ["places", "Customer (role customer, 1)", "Order (role orders, 0..*)", "Association"],
    ["receives", "Address (role address, 1)", "Order (role orders, 0..*)", "Association"],
    ["includes", "Order (role order, 1)", "OrderItem (role items, 1..*)", "Composition"],
    ["is sold in", "Product (role product, 1)", "OrderItem (role orderItems, 0..*)", "Association"],
    ["records", "Order (role order, 1)", "OrderEvent (role events, 1..*)", "Composition"],
    ["is paid by", "Order (role order, 1)", "Payment (role payments, 0..*)", "Association"],
    ["is shipped by", "Order (role order, 1)", "Shipping (role shipping, 0..1)", "Association"],
    ["is delivered to", "Address (role address, 1)", "Shipping (role shippings, 0..*)", "Association"],
], [2.6, 5.3, 5.6, 2.5], size=10)
p(f"Figure {nf()} shows where the inheritance setting is kept in VP. Open Specification of Product shows the tab ORM "
  "Class Detail, because the class has the stereotype «ORM Persistable»; its button Subclasses opens the dialog "
  "Inheritance Strategy, which lists the four kinds with their current strategy in brackets. I selected the four "
  "kinds, chose Table per subclass in the box at the bottom and pressed Apply.",
  keep=True)
figure("docs/evidence/vp_generation_v5/fig_table_per_subclass.png", "The inheritance setting of Product in VP: tab ORM Class Detail of its specification (left) and the dialog Inheritance Strategy with the four subclasses selected and Table per subclass chosen (right)", width_cm=15.5, max_h_cm=10)
p("Three of the names changed while I prepared the file. In the analysis diagram three associations were called "
  "contains; VP gives every relationship of the data model a unique name and wrote contains2 and contains3, so the "
  "order lines are now included in the order and the category tree reads \"is parent of\". The decimal attributes "
  "(the screen size of a laptop or a phone and the discount rate of a VIP) became Double, because VP maps a Decimal "
  "without a scale to decimal(19, 0), which would lose the digits after the point.")

# ======================================================================= 4.2
h2("4.2. Data Model")
h3("4.2.1. Tables Generated by Synchronize")
p("Synchronize to Entity Relationship Diagram creates one table for each class marked «ORM Persistable», one column "
  "for each attribute and one relationship with a foreign key for each association and each generalization. The "
  f"command takes three steps (Figure {nf()}):")
num(1, "Right-click the background of the class diagram and choose Synchronize to Entity Relationship Diagram (left "
       "of the figure).")
num(2, "The first dialog pairs the class diagram with the entity relationship diagram that will be created (top "
       "centre); OK.")
num(3, "The dialog Synchronize to Entity Relationship Diagram asks for the primary key of each table. VP proposes "
       "Auto Generate, which adds a column ID of its own (bottom centre). For each of the twelve classes that are not "
       "subclasses I chose the identifier attribute instead, ShippingId for Shipping, OrderId for Order and so on "
       "(right). The eight subclasses are not in the list: with Table per subclass their key is the key of the "
       "superclass. OK creates the diagram.")
F_SYNC = figure("docs/evidence/vp_generation_v5/fig_synchronize.png", "Synchronize to Entity Relationship Diagram: the command on the class diagram (left), the diagram it creates (top centre), the primary key that VP proposes (bottom centre) and the keys I chose (right)", width_cm=16.5, max_h_cm=12)
p(f"Figure {nf()} shows the result: twenty tables and twenty-two foreign keys. VP names a foreign key column after the "
  "referenced class and its key, for example CategoryCategoryId in Product. I renamed these columns to the name of "
  "the key they reference, CategoryId, and the key of the parent category to ParentId. I also renamed the table of "
  "Order to Orders, because ORDER is an SQL keyword, and arranged the tables on the page. VP copies the role names "
  "of the class diagram as the phrases of each relationship and places them over the table headers; the figure "
  "shows the relationship names only, and the role names stay in Table 4.1. Nothing else was edited by hand.")
landscape()
F_D2 = figure("vp/png/D2.png", "Data model generated from the class diagram by Synchronize to Entity Relationship Diagram: twenty tables, their keys and twenty-two relationships (diagram D2)", width_cm=26.5, max_h_cm=16)
portrait()
p(f"Table {nt()} gives the table, the primary key and the foreign keys of every class.", keep=True)
T_KEYS = table("Classes, tables, primary keys and foreign keys", [
    ["Class", "Table", "Primary key", "Foreign keys"],
    ["Category", "Category", "CategoryId", "ParentId to Category"],
    ["Product", "Product", "ProductId", "CategoryId to Category"],
    ["BookDetail, LaptopDetail, PhoneDetail, ApparelDetail", "the same names", "ProductId (also a foreign key to Product)", "none besides the key"],
    ["Customer", "Customer", "CustomerId", "none"],
    ["CustomerVIP, CustomerNew", "the same names", "CustomerId (also a foreign key to Customer)", "none besides the key"],
    ["Address", "Address", "AddressId", "CustomerId to Customer"],
    ["Staff", "Staff", "StaffId", "none"],
    ["Cart", "Cart", "CartId", "CustomerId to Customer"],
    ["CartItem", "CartItem", "CartItemId", "ProductId to Product, CartId to Cart"],
    ["Order", "Orders", "OrderId", "AddressId to Address, CustomerId to Customer"],
    ["OrderItem", "OrderItem", "OrderItemId", "ProductId to Product, OrderId to Orders"],
    ["OrderEvent", "OrderEvent", "EventId", "OrderId to Orders"],
    ["Payment", "Payment", "PaymentId", "OrderId to Orders"],
    ["PayCash, PayCredit", "the same names", "PaymentId (also a foreign key to Payment)", "none besides the key"],
    ["Shipping", "Shipping", "ShippingId", "AddressId to Address, OrderId to Orders"],
], [4.2, 2.6, 4.4, 4.8], size=10)
p("Types follow the class diagram: an Integer becomes integer(10), a String varchar(255), a Date date, a Double "
  "double(10) and a Boolean bit. A column is NOT NULL unless the value is optional: an attribute with multiplicity "
  "0..1, or a foreign key to a class whose end has multiplicity 0..1. Nineteen of the 122 columns allow NULL, and "
  f"Figure {F_D2} marks each of them with N: Category.ParentId and DetailKind; Product.Brand, Description and "
  "ImageFile; BookDetail.Publisher, Pages and Language; PhoneDetail.CameraMp and BatteryMah; ApparelDetail.Material; "
  "Customer.MiddleName, Phone and DateOfBirth; Address.HouseNumber; CustomerNew.WelcomeVoucher; OrderEvent.Note; "
  "PayCredit.ProviderToken; Shipping.DeliveredDate.")

h3("4.2.2. Product and Its Four Kinds")
p("Product is an ordinary class with four subclasses, and VP stores each subclass in its own table. The table has "
  "the attributes of that subclass only, and its key column ProductId is both the primary key of the table and a "
  "foreign key to Product: the Product row of a book and the row of the BookDetail table have the same key value. "
  "Customer and Payment are stored in the same way. Table " + nt() + " gives the reason for this choice.", keep=True)
table("Table per subclass for Product and its kinds", [
    ["", "Product and its four kinds"],
    ["Why", "A single Product table for all kinds would need fifteen more columns, and a book row would leave the eleven columns of laptops, phones and apparel empty. A table for each concrete kind would copy the Product columns four times, and CartItem and OrderItem would then need a foreign key for each kind."],
    ["How", "Product, BookDetail, LaptopDetail, PhoneDetail and ApparelDetail are five tables. A BookDetail row and its Product row share the same key value. CartItem and OrderItem keep one foreign key, ProductId, whatever the kind."],
    ["What if", "A fifth kind, such as toys, adds one class and one table. Product, CartItem and OrderItem do not change. The cost is one join when a page needs the attributes of a kind."],
], [2.2, 13.8], size=10.5)

h3("4.2.3. Why Each Attribute Is Stored")
p("Every attribute of the data model is persistent: it has a column, and a rule, a screen or a query reads it. "
  f"Table {nt()} gives the reason for the attributes whose purpose is not obvious from the name.", keep=True)
table("Attributes and what needs them", [
    ["Attribute", "Type", "Why it is stored"],
    ["Category.Slug", "String", "The key of a category in the address of its page and in the category menu, a short word without spaces or capitals (laptops, fiction)."],
    ["Category.DetailKind", "String, 0..1", "The kind of the products of the category (BOOK, LAPTOP, PHONE or APPAREL). The DAO reads it to build a BookDetail, a LaptopDetail and so on. A top-level category such as Books has none."],
    ["Product.Sku", "String", "The product code on the product page and in the stock list of the warehouse staff; unique (Section 5.3)."],
    ["Product.Status", "String", "BR01: only an ACTIVE product is sold. HIDDEN takes it off the shop and keeps it for the orders that have it."],
    ["Product.ImageFile", "String, 0..1", "The photo on the product page, and the catalogue image that the photo search compares (Chapter 3)."],
    ["Product.CreatedAt", "Date", "The sort order Newest of the catalogue."],
    ["Address.RecipientName, Phone", "String", "The person the carrier delivers to and calls, who need not be the customer."],
    ["Address.IsDefault", "Boolean", "The address chosen first at checkout."],
    ["CustomerVIP.DiscountRate", "Double", "BR04: the VIP discount of every order of the customer."],
    ["Staff.Role", "String", "BR13: SALES opens the order board, WAREHOUSE the stock list."],
    ["CartItem.CartPrice", "Integer", "BR03: the price the customer saw; checkout compares it with the current price."],
    ["Orders.PaymentMethod", "String", "The first status of the order: a card order starts in PENDING_PAYMENT, a cash order in PLACED (Table 1.9)."],
    ["Orders.Subtotal, Discount, ShippingFee, TotalAmount", "Integer", "BR04: the amounts the customer confirmed. A CHECK keeps the total equal to its parts."],
    ["OrderItem.ProductName, UnitPrice", "String; Integer", "BR03: the name and the price of the purchase, which stay when the catalogue changes."],
    ["OrderEvent.Actor, Note", "String", "BR09: who changed the status and why, shown in the history of the order."],
    ["Payment.Status", "String", "BR07, BR11: APPROVED or DECLINED from the provider, REFUNDED after a cancellation, COMPLETED for cash."],
    ["PayCredit.CardLast4, Expiry, ProviderToken", "String", "BR07: enough to show the card and to identify the payment at the provider. The full card number has no column."],
    ["PayCash.CashReceived, ChangeAmount", "Integer", "BR11: the cash collected on delivery and the change given back."],
    ["Shipping.Carrier, TrackingNumber", "String", "BR10: who delivers the parcel and the number the customer tracks it by; the tracking number is unique."],
], [4.6, 2.4, 9.0], size=10)

h3("4.2.4. Data Not Stored")
p("The search has no table. The text of a query, a photo, a spoken query (the browser turns it into text), the "
  "interpretation of the query, the scores and the list of results exist only while the request runs. The search "
  "indexes are kept in memory and rebuilt from the Product rows when the catalogue changes (Chapter 3), so the "
  "catalogue stays the only source. The totals of a cart are not stored either: the cart page computes them from "
  "the lines, and they are stored only in Orders, when the customer places the order.")

# ======================================================================= 4.3
h2("4.3. Data Model → DB")
p("The data model becomes the database with VP's Generate Database. With the Data Model file open, choose Tools, "
  f"DB, Generate Database. The dialog Database Generation of Figure {nf()} (left) opens. Generate Database is set to "
  "Create Database and Schema to <All>. Generate DDL is ticked and Export to database is not, so VP writes a DDL "
  "file and does not connect to a database server. The button Database Options opens the Database Configuration "
  "dialog (top right), where SQLite is the ticked database, so the DDL follows the SQLite dialect. The Output Path is "
  "the folder that receives the file. After OK, VP shows the progress dialog (bottom right) and writes one file with "
  "the extension .ddl, which I keep in the project as db/vp/vp_ddl.sql.")
F_GENDB = figure("docs/evidence/vp_generation_v5/fig_generate_database.png", "Generate Database: the options of the dialog (left), the SQLite choice of Database Options (top right) and the finished run (bottom right)", width_cm=15.5, max_h_cm=11)
p(f"Table {nt()} states what Generate Database writes for each element of the data model. Everything in the right "
  "column comes from the model; nothing in the DDL was typed by hand.", keep=True)
table("From the data model to the SQLite DDL that VP writes", [
    ["Data model element (D1, D2)", "What Generate Database writes", "Example in db/vp/vp_ddl.sql"],
    ["Class with «ORM Persistable», table of D2", "one CREATE TABLE with the name of the table", "CREATE TABLE Product (...)"],
    ["Identifier attribute of a class that is not a subclass", "INTEGER NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT", "ProductId, OrderId"],
    ["Subclass with Table per subclass", "the key of the superclass, both PRIMARY KEY and FOREIGN KEY", "BookDetail: PRIMARY KEY (ProductId), FOREIGN KEY(ProductId) REFERENCES Product(ProductId)"],
    ["Attribute type Integer, String, Date, Double, Boolean", "integer(10), varchar(255), date, double(10), integer(1) (bit in the ERD)", "Price integer(10); IsDefault integer(1)"],
    ["Attribute multiplicity 1 or 0..1", "NOT NULL, or no constraint", "Name varchar(255) NOT NULL; Brand varchar(255)"],
    ["Association end of multiplicity 1 or 0..1 on the other side", "a foreign key column, NOT NULL or nullable, and one FOREIGN KEY clause", "Product.CategoryId NOT NULL; Category.ParentId nullable"],
    ["Role names, association names, operations", "nothing: they stay in the model", ""],
    ["Business rules (a positive price, one cart per customer)", "nothing: the model cannot hold them; Section 5.3 adds them", ""],
], [4.6, 5.6, 5.8], size=10)
p("The file holds twenty CREATE TABLE statements, one for each table of the data model. The two tables of a "
  "purchase, copied from the file, show what VP writes: the key with AUTOINCREMENT, a type for every column, NOT NULL "
  "where the multiplicity is 1, and one FOREIGN KEY clause for each relationship. A foreign key column is NOT NULL "
  "because the end of the association has multiplicity 1.", keep=True)
code("""CREATE TABLE Orders (OrderId INTEGER NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT, AddressId integer(10) NOT NULL,
  CustomerId integer(10) NOT NULL, OrderDate date NOT NULL, Status varchar(255) NOT NULL,
  PaymentMethod varchar(255) NOT NULL, Subtotal integer(10) NOT NULL, Discount integer(10) NOT NULL,
  ShippingFee integer(10) NOT NULL, TotalAmount integer(10) NOT NULL,
  FOREIGN KEY(CustomerId) REFERENCES Customer(CustomerId), FOREIGN KEY(AddressId) REFERENCES Address(AddressId));
CREATE TABLE OrderItem (OrderItemId INTEGER NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT, ProductId integer(10) NOT NULL,
  OrderId integer(10) NOT NULL, ProductName varchar(255) NOT NULL, Quantity integer(10) NOT NULL,
  UnitPrice integer(10) NOT NULL, FOREIGN KEY(OrderId) REFERENCES Orders(OrderId),
  FOREIGN KEY(ProductId) REFERENCES Product(ProductId));""", size=8)
p("VP writes keys, types and NOT NULL only. The data model holds no rule such as \"the price is positive\" or \"a "
  "product appears once in a cart\", so the DDL has no CHECK, no UNIQUE and no delete rule. The schema of the running "
  "shop is this DDL with those rules added by hand; Section 5.3 shows them and the script that checks that the shop's "
  "database still has exactly the generated tables, columns and keys.")

# ======================================================================= 4.4
h2("4.4. Normalization")
p(f"The tables of Figure {F_D2} are in third normal form, with the exceptions that Table {nt()} names.", keep=True)
table("Normal forms and the data model", [
    ["Form", "What the model does", "Where to see it"],
    ["1NF", "Every column holds one value. Repeating groups have tables of their own: the lines of a cart and of an order, the events of an order, the addresses of a customer.", "CartItem, OrderItem, OrderEvent, Address"],
    ["2NF", "Every table has a single-column primary key, so no column can depend on part of a key.", "Table 4.2"],
    ["3NF", "Every column depends on the key and on nothing else, with two intended exceptions. OrderItem keeps the product name and the unit price of the purchase, copies of Product on purpose, because a past order must not change when the catalogue does (BR03). Orders keeps the subtotal, discount, shipping fee and total, which can be computed from the lines; they are the amounts the customer confirmed, and a CHECK keeps the total equal to subtotal minus discount plus fee.", "OrderItem and Orders tables"],
], [1.6, 10.4, 4.0], size=10)
p("Take one table with a row for each ordered item instead of Orders and OrderItem. It would repeat the order date, "
  "the status, the address and the totals on every row, and changing the status of one order would need an update on "
  "every row of that order. Orders and OrderItem are separate tables for this reason: the order data is stored once, "
  "and each item row stores only what belongs to the item.")

h2("4.5. Chapter Summary")
p("The Data Model file adds types, identifiers, multiplicities, role names, «ORM Persistable» and Table per subclass "
  "to the twenty analysis classes. Synchronize to Entity Relationship Diagram turned them into twenty tables and "
  "twenty-two foreign keys, and Generate Database turned the tables into an SQLite DDL. The only hand edits are the "
  "names of the foreign key columns, the name Orders and the layout of the page; the business rules that a data "
  "model cannot express are added in the design, in Section 5.3.")
